Subject: QUANTUM: Suggestion

Robin Hanson writes:
> Ray Cromwell writes:

> >I am not against _strict_ interpretations of mathematics that are testable
> >or best (according to Occam), I am against creative interpretations (e.g.
> >Von Neumann's "... and then a miracle happens."). However, I do feel that
> >sometimes even strict interpretations get in the way of further scientific
> >breakthoughs.
> 
> Huh?  Are you arguing against all use of intutition, or only against the use
> of intuition in philosophy?  Or are you arguing against all inferences which
> are not currently and stricly testable?  These all sound like pretty
> indefensible positions to me.

  I am against giving too much weight to philosophical models based on 
mathematical interpretations. They are useful for visualizing and
exploring the outer reaches of theory, but unless they are testable
they can't be considered "true".  Whether you believe in Many Worlds,
Copenhagen, or any of the interpretations seems to be a matter of
faith.

  I didn't say I was against all use of intuition. I was merely trying to
point out an inconsistency in Dale's post. He said interpretations
should be based on mathematical content not the "reasonable" because
our intuition has been shown to be wrong in the past. I was trying to
point out that interpretations based on mathematical content are
also subject to flawed intuition. Thus, if two different theories
explain the same experimental data indentically using two different
techniques (say, fields vs particle exchange), I would argue that
the real picture is probably neither and that there is a deeper
connection we are probably missing.

  I am not against theories which aren't currently testable, rather ones
that aren't testable period. And when I say I am against them, I mean
that we should try to find something better if possible.

> Robin Hanson
