Date: Tue, 9 Mar 93 11:04:32 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON/ALIEN: Invasion of the Self-Owned Organizations

Some people are afraid that AIs will outcompete us and take over our
economy.  Others have feared such things from ETs or from those of other
races.  These seem to be common human fears, well represented in SF and
other fiction.

Yet, it has occurred to me that even without technical breakthroughs, a
much more mundane alien entity could take over: self-owned organizations.
If someone could create such an organization, endow it with a big pot of
money, and then have it designed interally so as to reliably hire agents
who simply invest this money for long-term growth, then this organization
should grow faster than the economy as a whole.  After all, long term (log
average) rates of return have been consistently higher than the growth rate
of the world economy.  Eventually when such organizations came to dominate
the economy, rates of return would fall to the economies growth rate (and
indirectly spurring that growth rate).

The creator of such an organization might be able to create latent goals,
which only come into effect after the organization has grown to a certain
percentage of the world economy, or when some other trigger was hit.  Who
knows what strange creatures might thereby be created.

Of course designing organizations isn't trivial, but it isn't anywhere near
as hard as designing an AI.  But maybe the reason no such organizations
have arisen so far is that no one has successfully designed such an
organization - it being rather hard to keep the human agents of the
organization from stealing its wealth over the long term.

Or maybe the problem is that people have anticipated this possibility, and
used the coercive power of states to prevent such growth.  In the US, I
think non-profits by law can't reinvest returns from investments, and
for-profits can't be self-owned (or can they?).  Is this unfair hindering
of our true "mind children"?  Or are states the real self-owned
organizations, forcing people within their power to attend to the state's
long-term growth goals against those people's preferences, and tricking
those people into thinking they are active owners?

Robin Hanson

