With this I must beg to differ.  Sure, designing a small organization
isn't that tough... but an AI for an ant wouldn't be either.  Besides
the fact, that in designing an organization it is inherent that you
must understand and be able to motivate many separate intelligences.
Designing such an organization that could stand the test of time would
be pretty damned hard.  

The big conceptual difference between AI and organizations, is that in
AI I assume everything is spelled out specifically... whereas in 
long lived organizations specifics mean inflexible mean short lived
organization.  On the other hand, nonspecific means unfocused means
no goals/uncompetitive or that the organization will be abused by the
interests of opportunistic individuals.

The only organization I could see that could survive that long would
have to be really amorphous... something like the pooled interests of
a group of highly skilled individuals.  The large the organization gets,
the more individual interests it would reflect... until it really 
wouldn't reflect much of anything at all.  Chaos and anarchy... not
bad.

