Date: Tue, 9 Mar 93 11:54:46 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON/AI: What if AIs can't catch up?

Just because a technology seems to be superior on some intrinsic
grounds doesn't mean it will win in the marketplace.  A lot depends on
how and when it is introduced, and on its connections to the rest of
the economy.  

I'm sure Tim May could tell us about chip or memory technology that
looked better on some fundamental physical grounds, but just couldn't
catch up with the larger investment made in the standard technology.
By the time tests had shown that 10x improvement with this alternative
technology, more mundane evolution had improved the standard
technology by 20x.  Of course similar mundane evolution would have
improved the alternative by a similar factor, *if* as much attention
had been paid to it.  But it wasn't.  

Or consider all those great designed programming languages.  I'm sure
Mark Miller could tell us all how much better Smalltalk is than C, and
was developed before I think, but C has won for now.  A worse language
with a bigger installed base of programmers, programs, etc. beats a
theoretically better language with less allegiance.  C may be modified
slowly to borrow good features of Smalltalk, as in C++, but if a
feature is hard to graft on, it may just lose.  

Or imagine an existing city at a certain location, even though a
location upstream is now better from the view of current technology -
closer to raw materials, ease of building transportation, suitability
of ground for building, the weather, etc.  While a new city may start
up there and gradually take business away from the old one, the old
city may "fill the niche" and prevent the new city from forming.  All
that existing investment in the old location may not be worth dumping
due to the cost of moving.  And even if it were worth it no
organization may be able to feasibly buy up the old city, move it, and
resell it for a profit.  And no one business may find it in their
interest to move without an agreement from all the others to do so as
well.  Businesses want to be near each other.

Now consider AI.  Part of the essence of standard visions of AI, it
seems to me, is of a tightly-connected set of software modules
autonomous from people, that is only loosely connected to any one
human.  In contrast, the connectivity of most current software is
better described as radiating away from various hubs of human minds.
Humans hold high level models of the software in their minds, write
it, and modify it.  And most software in use sits around people
waiting to be used at the direction of those people, with subroutines
farther away from human requests more changeable because they are less
densely connected with other software and constraints.

That is, most software is now like suburbs collected around city hubs
of human minds, while an AI vision imagines new hubs with software at
their core as well as at their periphery.  This is a self-consistent
vision, but if people keep getting better then even intrinsically
better AIs may not be able to bump people from their cognitive task
niches.  There may be just too much investment in making other
software well matched to work with human minds.

Of course if the difference is many orders of magnitude in efficiency,
then even big change can happen.  Cars won over horses, etc.  But how
much better than people is AI intrinsically?  Well one might imagine
that in principle their hardware can be evolved much further, based as
it is on silicon, etc. and not on biology.  But if people were to
"upload", moving their software over to the same type of hardware,
then the contest would be more equal.  And difficult as such uploading
might be, I would argue it is probably still much easier than doing AI
well.

So with uploaded people there before competitive AIs arrive on the
scene, the AIs would have to win on the grounds of not needing to use
spatial metaphors and mappings as much, not tied to archaic emotions,
not demanding time for recreation, etc.  But with enough of a head
start, enough investments could be made in trying to deal with these
limitations that AI just couldn't catch up.

What if the answer isn't determined yet, because its near a balance
and things we do now could tip it one way or another?  Then we have to
ask: Do we want AIs to outcompete us?  If the answer is no, then we
should tip the balance against them.  Yet our main balance-tipping
actions, funding much AI research with little prospect of commercial
profit yet little research into uploading, push prospects in the other
direction.

Thus the naive person-on-the-streets qualms about funding AI research
may be spot on - why do we want to *help* them take over?

Robin Hanson (AI researcher :-)

