From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: ECON/ALIEN: Invasion of the Self-Owned Organizations

   From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>

   Of course designing organizations isn't trivial, but it isn't anywhere near
   as hard as designing an AI.  But maybe the reason no such organizations
   have arisen so far is that no one has successfully designed such an
   organization - it being rather hard to keep the human agents of the
   organization from stealing its wealth over the long term.

I've wondered how to build such an organization, but in the context of
building a long-lasting (thousands of years) secret society or
conspiracy.  On top of the practical difficulty, our present culture
defines that all organizations are ultimately controlled by a group of
people -- partnerships by their partners, corporations by their
stockholders, trusts by their trustees, non-profits by their boards of
directors or members (depending on how its organized), etc.  (And
governments by voters.)  There is no provision for an organization
that is not controlled by a group of humans.  I've seen it claimed
that if a group of corporations got into a circular-ownership
situation such that no humans were present as owners, the State would
step in and seize the whole lot as "abandoned property".

A few years ago there was a takeover battle in which two corporations
attempted to buy up each other.  Unfortunately, it would not have
resulted in self-ownership, since if either succeeded, it would have
replaced the board of the other and terminated the counterattacking
acquisition effort.

Though you probably could get away with self-ownership if you had
several corporations owning each other circularly, since nobody would
ever notice the loop.

Dale

