Date: Tue, 9 Mar 1993 16:06-EST
From: Marc.Ringuette@GS80.SP.CS.CMU.EDU
Subject: ECON/AI: What if AIs can't catch up?

Robin suggests some kind of "AI desert" because software is typically
designed around a human operator.  Such a desert most emphatically does not
exist; in fact, much the opposite.  Every little bit of progress towards
autonomous machines will yield large profit-making opportunities.

Robin wonders whether software-enhanced humans will outperform AI's and
therefore inhibit their growth.  But there's one key area -- cost -- in which
AI's will clearly outperform the humans.  It's also true that interfaces
between humans and computers are cumbersome and will remain far worse than
interfaces between programs.  This is another competitive advantage of AI's.

Robin's worries are unfounded.


-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu)

