Date: Tue, 9 Mar 93 15:08:27 CST
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: ECON/AI: What if AIs can't catch up?

Robin Hanson writes:

> So with uploaded people there before competitive AIs arrive on the
> scene, the AIs would have to win on the grounds of not needing to use
> spatial metaphors and mappings as much, not tied to archaic emotions,
> not demanding time for recreation, etc.  But with enough of a head
> start, enough investments could be made in trying to deal with these
> limitations that AI just couldn't catch up.

Interesting speculation.  The major advantage of artificially
intelligent artifacts in a world of "abundance" is that they
don't need to be paid for their thinking time (assuming that
AI ownership is normal).  However, in the full-blown uploading
scenario, I could make copies of myself (or purchase copies of
others) to do such tasks.  It's not immediately clear to me which
scenario is more attractive.

The lines get even blurrier when we start to talk of merging
uploaded (or even in vivo) with AI components (which would
surely happen).  Maybe as both uploading and AI become feasible,
the distinctions will not be meaningful.

My intuition, though, is that AI (that is, intelligence constructed
without the goal of low-level functional duplication of physiological
structures) will always remain more capable at useful tasks than
uploads (intelligence constructed as duplication of physiology) with
a given amount of raw materials, simply because it is designed to
be useful.

If anybody disagrees with this projection, any idea when uploads
will surpass AIs in ability? [even today's idiot AIs are better than
the virtually nonexistent uploads].

Still, given a choice between one or the other, I'd certainly
pick uploading!

derek
