From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: Responding to Ancient Messages

(Note for the yogically and reincarnationally inclined: by "ancient
messages" I mean messages from this list that are several months or so old,
not messages from the Civil War or the authors of the Vedas and
Upanishads.)

Two questions:

1. Is it considered a list rule violation to quote from and respond to a
very old message? (Inasmuch as it thus exposes the message to people who
were not on the list at the time the original message was posted. Note that
this happens at some level all the time.)

(In the extreme case of quoting the entire article, which is considered
tacky, but is not a formal violation of list rules, then this effectively
reposts the article. What's the ruling on this?)

2. Do the readers consider this desirable?

I bring this up because I've been making much more use of "Eudora," an
offline mail reader for the Macintosh (highest recommendations!), and it
makes it very easy for me to sort and filter past messages into dozens of
folders. For example, I've created a subfolder for certain topics and all
the related messages. In doing this, I was motivated to write a response to
a thread, even though the thread is several months old.

Certainly the best time to respond to a message is when it's fresh and
current. But new ideas, or new syntheses with other threads, can occur.

I expect others have similar schemes for dealing with mail messages, beyond
simple chronological sorting, and that this issue may be of interest to
others.

Any opinions?

-Tim



